course: dictionary
lesson: 5011
title: Infinite: no end, no limit
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: You may meet the word infinite in maths, in a story, or in an advert. Its context gives the clues. Infinite means without any end or limit, so big it can never be counted. «There are infinite numbers: whatever number you pick, you can always add 1.» In maths, that is exactly what it means.
explain: In everyday talk, people stretch the word to mean extremely great. «I have infinite homework» does not mean the homework will go on for ever. It means there is a huge amount. Ask yourself: is this sentence about maths, or is someone exaggerating? The clues tell you which meaning fits.
explain: Be careful with «INFINITE COINS!!!» in a link on a screen. The clues are the excited shouting and the warning from Leo's dad about links that steal your account. Here infinite is bait, meant to make you click. When a promise sounds too good to be true, check the context before you trust it.
fact: Infinite means without any end or limit.
fact: «Infinite patience» means extremely great patience, with no limit.
fact: In maths, an infinite line or set goes on for ever, like the counting numbers 1, 2, 3, ….
fact: The symbol for infinity is ∞.
fact: People use «infinite» to exaggerate, as in «infinite homework», but a line segment is not infinite because it has two ends.
exercise: «There are infinitely many numbers.» What does infinite mean in maths? | *Going on for ever, with no end | Hard to read | Exactly ten | Very small
exercise: «I have infinite homework.» What does infinite mean here? | Lost in the bag | Exactly one page | *Extremely great, a huge amount | Finished at last
exercise: Find the symbol for infinity. | # | & | % | *∞
test: Mia says a line that goes on for ever is infinite. Is she right? | No, every line always stops | Infinite lines only exist in games | *Yes, a line with no end is infinite | Only lines drawn in pencil are infinite
test: «INFINITE COINS!!!» appears in a link on a screen. What is the context telling you? | A maths lesson about counting | A real free gift from the shop | *A possible trick or scam that wants you to click | A new level in a game you already own
exam: Which meaning best fits infinite in «She showed infinite patience with the class»? | *Extremely great, with no limit | Not at all patient | Quick and loud | Very limited and short
